    "['no shutdown', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'delete interfaces GigabitEthernet1/0/0 disable', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\ndelete interfaces GigabitEthernet1/0/0 disable\\ndelete interfaces GigabitEthernet2/0/0 disable\\n##\\n\\nExplanation: In Juniper\\'s Junos OS, the equivalent of Cisco\\'s \"no shutdown\" is to remove the \"disable\" flag from the interface configuration. The \"delete\" command is used to remove configuration statements, effectively enabling the interface. \\n\\nNote: The exact interface names may differ between Cisco and Juniper (e.g., \"ge-1/0/0\" instead of \"GigabitEthernet1/0/0\"), but I\\'ve kept the Cisco naming for consistency with the original configuration. In a real translation, you would adjust the interface naming to match Juniper\\'s conventions.', 'trans_template': 'delete interfaces GigabitEthernet1/0/0 disable'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'delete interfaces GigabitEthernet2/0/0 disable', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\ndelete interfaces GigabitEthernet1/0/0 disable\\ndelete interfaces GigabitEthernet2/0/0 disable\\n##\\n\\nExplanation: In Juniper\\'s Junos OS, the equivalent of Cisco\\'s \"no shutdown\" is to remove the \"disable\" flag from the interface configuration. The \"delete\" command is used to remove configuration statements, effectively enabling the interface. \\n\\nNote: The exact interface names may differ between Cisco and Juniper (e.g., \"ge-1/0/0\" instead of \"GigabitEthernet1/0/0\"), but I\\'ve kept the Cisco naming for consistency with the original configuration. In a real translation, you would adjust the interface naming to match Juniper\\'s conventions.', 'trans_template': 'delete interfaces GigabitEthernet2/0/0 disable'}]]": 1,
    "['ipv6 address [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6 address 2001:DB8:3::2/64', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco `ipv6 address` command:\\n\\n##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6 address 2001:DB8:3::2/64\\n##\\n\\nThis command:\\n1. Specifies the GigabitEthernet1/0/0 interface (ge-1/0/0 in Juniper)\\n2. Configures unit 0 (Juniper's default logical unit)\\n3. Enables IPv6 address family (family inet6)\\n4. Sets the same IPv6 address and prefix length (2001:DB8:3::2/64)\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6 address 2001:DB8:3::2/64'}]]": 1,
    "['ipv6 router [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco `ipv6 router isis` command:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0\\nset protocols isis interface ge-2/0/0\\n##\\n\\nNote:\\n1. In Juniper, you enable IS-IS per interface under the protocols hierarchy\\n2. The command needs to be applied to both interfaces (ge-1/0/0 and ge-2/0/0 in Juniper's naming convention)\\n3. Unlike Cisco's interface-level command, Juniper uses a global configuration approach where you specify which interfaces participate in IS-IS\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols isis interface ge-2/0/0', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco `ipv6 router isis` command:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0\\nset protocols isis interface ge-2/0/0\\n##\\n\\nNote:\\n1. In Juniper, you enable IS-IS per interface under the protocols hierarchy\\n2. The command needs to be applied to both interfaces (ge-1/0/0 and ge-2/0/0 in Juniper's naming convention)\\n3. Unlike Cisco's interface-level command, Juniper uses a global configuration approach where you specify which interfaces participate in IS-IS\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols isis interface ge-2/0/0'}]]": 1,
    "['isis circuit-type [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols isis interface <interface-name> level 2 disable', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco `isis circuit-type level-2-only` command:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols isis interface <interface-name> level 2 disable\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, you explicitly disable level 1 (making it level-2-only) rather than specifying level-2-only directly. Replace `<interface-name>` with the actual interface name (e.g., `ge-1/0/0.0`). The Juniper command also requires the interface to be specified in unit notation (usually `.0`).\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols isis interface <interface-name> level 2 disable'}]]": 1,
    "['no shutdown', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 disable false', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 disable false\\nset interfaces ge-2/0/0 disable false\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\nIn Juniper, the equivalent of Cisco's `no shutdown` is `disable false` (or simply omitting the `disable` statement, as interfaces are enabled by default). The command must be applied under the specific interface hierarchy, hence the `set interfaces <interface-name>` prefix. I've included both interface configurations since both had `no shutdown` in the original Cisco config.\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 disable false'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-2/0/0 disable false', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 disable false\\nset interfaces ge-2/0/0 disable false\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\nIn Juniper, the equivalent of Cisco's `no shutdown` is `disable false` (or simply omitting the `disable` statement, as interfaces are enabled by default). The command must be applied under the specific interface hierarchy, hence the `set interfaces <interface-name>` prefix. I've included both interface configurations since both had `no shutdown` in the original Cisco config.\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-2/0/0 disable false'}]]": 1,
    "['ipv6 address [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet6 address 2001:db8:4::1/64', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco `ipv6 address` command:\\n\\n##\\nset interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet6 address 2001:db8:4::1/64\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. Juniper uses `ge-` prefix instead of `GigabitEthernet`\\n2. Juniper requires specifying `unit 0` and `family inet6`\\n3. Juniper commands are case-sensitive for addresses (lowercase)\\n4. The interface numbering follows Juniper's format (ge-2/0/0 instead of GigabitEthernet2/0/0)\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet6 address 2001:db8:4::1/64'}]]": 1,
    "['ipv6 router [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols isis interface <interface-name> ipv6-adjacency-segment', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent command for `ipv6 router isis`:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols isis interface <interface-name> ipv6-adjacency-segment\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, you would replace `<interface-name>` with the actual interface (e.g., `ge-1/0/0` or `ge-2/0/0`). The `ipv6-adjacency-segment` enables IS-IS for IPv6 on that interface, which is the equivalent functionality to Cisco's `ipv6 router isis` command.\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols isis interface <interface-name> ipv6-adjacency-segment'}]]": 1,
    "['isis circuit-type [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols isis interface <interface-name> level 2 disable', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the equivalent Juniper configuration command for the Cisco `isis circuit-type level-2-only`:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols isis interface <interface-name> level 2 disable\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, you would need to specify the actual interface name (e.g., `ge-1/0/0.0`) in place of `<interface-name>`. The command disables level 1, effectively making it level-2-only, similar to the Cisco configuration.\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols isis interface <interface-name> level 2 disable'}]]": 1,
